{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Before YHWH your Elohim you shall eat it.</b> He is speaking of the priest — for we have already found that it is one of the priestly gifts, whether whole or blemished, as it says ״וּבְשָׂרָם יִהְיֶה לָךְ״ (Numbers 18), etc. (Bekhorot 28)." "<b>Year by year.</b> From here we learn that one does not hold it over beyond its year. One might think it becomes disqualified once its year has passed — but it has already been compared to tithe, as it says ״וְאָכַלְתָּ לִפְנֵי יְיָ אֱלֹהֶיךָ מַעְשַׂר דְּגָנְךָ תִּירֹשְׁךָ וְיִצְהָרֶךָ וּבְכֹרֹת בְּקָרְךָ וְצֹאנֶךָ״ (Deuteronomy 14): just as second tithe is not disqualified from one year to the next, so too the firstborn is not disqualified — but the mitzvah is within its year (Bekhorot 27)." "<b>Year by year.</b> If one slaughtered it at the end of its year, one may eat it that day and one day into the following year — this teaches that it may be eaten for two days and one night (ibid.)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {1 "The divine name in the quoted verse from Deuteronomy 14 is rendered with standard abbreviation יְיָ as it appears in the Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:47:18.231500282Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}